        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Topical sealers sit on top of concrete and eventually fail visibly. Penetrating sealers go inside it and fail invisibly. If you have ever stripped a flaking acrylic sealer off a driveway, you already understand why that difference matters.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. How Penetrating Sealers Work
        </h2>

        <p>
          Concrete looks like a solid material and it simply is not. A cured slab contains a connected network of capillary pores, and water moves through that network freely — which is how a driveway absorbs rain, how road salt reaches the reinforcing steel, and how a slab comes to hold enough water that a hard freeze can break it apart from inside.
        </p>

        <p>
          A topical sealer addresses this by covering the surface, so water never reaches the pores. That works while the film is intact, and stops working as soon as it is not.
        </p>

        <p>
          A penetrating sealer works differently. It is carried in a solvent or in water, soaks into the capillary network, and then reacts chemically with the silica in the concrete to form a permanent hydrophobic lining inside the pores. The pores stay open — the slab can still breathe and release vapour — but their walls now repel water rather than drawing it in.
        </p>

        <p>
          Two consequences follow, and they are the entire argument for the category.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>There is nothing on the surface to fail.</strong> No film means nothing to peel, blister, wear through in a traffic lane, scratch, turn white under UV or become slippery when wet. The surface texture and slip resistance of the concrete are unchanged because you have not added anything to the surface at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The slab can still dry.</strong> Because the pores remain open to vapour, moisture arriving from below can escape. This is why penetrating sealers are far more forgiving on slabs without a vapour barrier, where a film-forming sealer would blister as vapour pushed up beneath it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The cost of all this is that a penetrating sealer does nothing for appearance. It will not darken concrete, will not add gloss, will not produce the wet look people want on pavers, and will not hide staining. If appearance is your objective, this is the wrong category.
        </p>

        <h2 id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Silane, Siloxane and Silicate
        </h2>

        <p>
          Three chemistries are sold as penetrating sealers and they do genuinely different jobs. Confusing one for another is by some distance the most common buying error made in this whole category, and it is worth a minute to get straight.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silane</h3>
        <p>
          The smallest molecule of the three, which lets it penetrate dense, tight concrete that larger molecules cannot enter. Deep penetration means the water-repellent layer sits well below the wearing surface, so abrasion at the top of the slab does not remove the protection. The trade-off is that silanes are volatile — a meaningful proportion evaporates before it reacts — which is why they are usually blended rather than used alone.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Siloxane</h3>
        <p>
          Larger molecules that penetrate less deeply but are far less volatile, so more of what you apply stays and reacts. They perform excellently on porous, open substrates — older concrete, brick, block and pavers — where the pore structure is wide enough to admit them.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silane-Siloxane Blends</h3>
        <p>
          Which is why nearly every quality product is a blend. Real concrete does not have one pore size; it has a distribution. A blend gives you small molecules for the tight pores and larger ones for the open pores, and it is the correct default for almost any exterior slab.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Silicates and Densifiers</h3>
        <p>
          A different mechanism entirely. Sodium, potassium or lithium silicates react with free lime in the concrete to create additional binder inside the pore structure, physically hardening the slab and stopping the surface dusting. They tighten the surface but do not make it hydrophobic, so their water repellency is modest.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical distinction: <strong>use a silane-siloxane when the problem is water, salt or freeze-thaw damage. Use a silicate densifier when the problem is a soft, dusting slab.</strong> On a garage floor that both dusts and gets wet, professionals apply the densifier first and the repellent over it.
        </p>


        <!-- Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
