                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2 id="vs-topical" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Penetrating vs Topical: An Honest Comparison
        </h2>

        <p>
          Both categories have a legitimate place, and the choice comes down to whether you are protecting the concrete or decorating it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Service life</strong> is the clearest difference. A quality penetrating sealer gives seven to ten years on a driveway. A topical acrylic gives two to three before it needs recoating, and each recoat eventually requires stripping the accumulated layers. Over twenty years that is a very different amount of work.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Failure mode</strong> is the second. When a penetrating sealer reaches the end of its life, water starts being absorbed again and nothing looks different. When a topical sealer fails, it does so visibly — peeling, flaking, blushing white where moisture is trapped beneath it, or wearing into obvious traffic lanes.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Appearance</strong> is where topicals win outright. If you want pavers to look permanently wet, or a stained patio to have depth and gloss, only a film can do that. No penetrating sealer will ever produce that effect, and products claiming to be both are usually a film with better marketing.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Safety</strong> favours penetrating chemistry. A glossy acrylic on a pool deck or an entry is slippery when wet unless grit is added to it. A penetrating sealer leaves the original texture and therefore the original traction.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Stain resistance</strong> favours topicals, with one exception. A film physically blocks spills from reaching the concrete, so it resists staining better than most penetrating sealers. The exception is products with genuine oil repellency, which close much of that gap.
        </p>

        <p>
          One compatibility warning worth repeating: you generally cannot apply a topical sealer over a penetrating one. The water repellency you have just built into the surface is exactly what prevents a water-based coating from bonding. Decide before you seal, not after.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Where to Use Them
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Driveways</h3>
        <p>
          The strongest case in the whole category. Driveways face freeze-thaw cycling, deicing salt, oil drips and constant abrasion — everything that destroys a film. A silane-siloxane protects against the water and chloride intrusion that actually damages the slab, and there is nothing on the surface for tyres to scuff off.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garage Floors</h3>
        <p>
          Good, with a caveat. Penetrating sealers handle the moisture and the salt brine that drips off a car in winter, and they will not lift under hot tyres the way some coatings do. But they do not resist oil staining well unless you specifically buy a product with oil repellency, and they will not give you the showroom look that an epoxy or polyaspartic coating does.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patios and Walkways</h3>
        <p>
          Excellent, particularly where slip resistance matters. A penetrating sealer keeps the surface exactly as grippy as it was, which is more than can be said for a gloss acrylic on a wet path.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pavers</h3>
        <p>
          Genuinely good if you want the pavers to keep looking natural, and the right answer where efflorescence has been a problem — because the joints stay open and the slab can dry. If you want the wet look, you want a topical, and our <a href="/best-paver-sealers-for-a-wet-look/">wet look paver sealer guide</a> covers that instead.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basement Floors and Walls</h3>
        <p>
          Useful, and more forgiving than a film where there is vapour drive from below. On a wall with real hydrostatic pressure, though, a penetrating sealer is not the answer — that needs a waterproofing system and, more importantly, drainage work outside.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coverage and What It Costs</h3>

        <p>
          Coverage varies more with the concrete than with the product, which makes tin figures unusually unreliable here. A dense, power-trowelled garage slab may take only 250 to 300 square feet per gallon per coat because there is little pore volume to fill. An old, weathered broom-finished driveway can take twice as much, and exposed aggregate or porous block will drink considerably more still.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical approach is to seal a small measured area first, note how much you used, and scale from there before buying the rest. Running out partway through a driveway is a genuine problem, because the lap line between a sealed area and one sealed a week later is sometimes visible after rain.
        </p>

        <p>
          On cost, penetrating sealers look expensive per gallon and are usually cheaper per year of service. A premium silane-siloxane at seven to ten years against a topical acrylic at two to three means roughly three applications avoided, plus the stripping that eventually accompanies a layered film. For a driveway that arithmetic strongly favours the penetrating option even before the labour is counted.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. How to Apply One
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Wait for the concrete to cure.</strong> New concrete needs a full 28 days. Sealing earlier interferes with the curing chemistry and the sealer cannot penetrate properly into a slab that is still hydrating.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean thoroughly and remove any existing sealer.</strong> A penetrating sealer must reach bare concrete. If an old acrylic film is present, even a worn one, it has to come off — the new sealer cannot pass through it. Degrease oil spots specifically, since oil blocks penetration completely.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Let it dry properly.</strong> Twenty-four hours minimum after washing, and longer in cool or humid weather. Damp pores are full pores, and a sealer applied to them sits on the surface instead of going in.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Apply evenly and do not over-apply.</strong> A low-pressure pump sprayer is the standard tool, with a roller or brush to work it in on textured surfaces. The concrete should look uniformly wet, not flooded. Excess sealer has nowhere to go once the pores are saturated and will dry as a haze.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Follow the two-coat instruction where it exists.</strong> Most quality products specify two coats applied wet-on-wet, typically within a short window. This is not optional — the second coat is what fills what the first did not reach.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Work systematically, because you cannot see what you have done.</strong> This is the practical difficulty unique to penetrating sealers. Mark out sections, keep track of them, and overlap deliberately. A missed strip on a driveway will announce itself the first time it rains, as a dark band where water is being absorbed.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep off it while it cures.</strong> Foot traffic after four to six hours is typically fine and vehicles after 24 to 48, but check the label. Full water repellency usually develops over several days as the chemical reaction completes.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Expecting a wet look</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Penetrating sealers are invisible by design. If you want darkened, glossy concrete you want a topical acrylic or urethane, and you should accept the maintenance that comes with it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Applying to wet or green concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The sealer needs open, dry pore structure to enter. Damp concrete blocks it, and new concrete needs a full 28-day cure before anything seals it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Over-applying and leaving puddles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Excess sealer cannot penetrate further once the pores are full. Left to dry it forms a hazy residue on the surface — the one way a penetrating sealer can look bad.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using a densifier as a water repellent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Silicate densifiers harden and dust-proof concrete but provide only limited water repellency. If moisture is the problem, you need a silane or siloxane, sometimes over a densifier.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing a slab with active moisture from below</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A slab without a vapour barrier pushes moisture up continuously. A penetrating sealer is more tolerant of this than a film, but heavy vapour drive still needs solving at the source.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a penetrating concrete sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A sealer that soaks into the concrete and reacts chemically inside the pore structure rather than forming a film on the surface. It repels water from within the slab, so there is no coating to peel, wear through, discolour or become slippery.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a penetrating concrete sealer last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Typically seven to ten years for a quality silane-siloxane on a driveway, and longer on vertical or sheltered surfaces. That compares with roughly two to three years for a topical acrylic, which is the main argument for penetrating chemistry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do penetrating sealers change the appearance of concrete?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Almost none. A good silane-siloxane leaves the slab looking exactly as it did — same colour, same sheen, same texture and same slip resistance. Some products darken very slightly on porous concrete, which is why a test patch is worth doing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Silane or siloxane — which is better?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Most quality products blend both. Silane molecules are smaller and penetrate dense concrete more deeply; siloxane molecules are larger and perform better on porous, open surfaces. A blend covers the range of pore sizes in real concrete.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a topical sealer over a penetrating one?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Generally no. The penetrating sealer makes the surface water-repellent, which is precisely what stops a water-based topical sealer from bonding. If you may want a decorative finish later, apply that first and use the penetrating sealer only where you are sure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a penetrating sealer stop my concrete spalling?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">It substantially reduces the risk, because spalling is caused by water freezing inside the slab. Less water absorbed means less to freeze. It cannot repair existing spalling, and it cannot help if deicing salt is already driving damage.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to reapply penetrating sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Eventually, though far less often than a topical. Test annually by sprinkling water on the surface: while it beads, the sealer is working. When it soaks in and darkens the concrete, it is time to reapply.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Are penetrating sealers safe for pavers and stone?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, and they are usually the better choice there. Because they leave no film, they do not trap moisture in the joints or make paver surfaces slippery, and they will not turn white the way a failing acrylic does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

